  "note": "紅茶の雑学: milk tea (brew tea, add milk after) vs royal milk tea (leaves simmered in milk, little water). Name is Japanese-coined (和製英語), c. 1965 Kyoto tea shop — hedged. Readings: 茶葉→ちゃば, 淹れた→いれた, 別物→べつもの, 1965→せんきゅうひゃくろくじゅうご.",
  "sources": [
    "macaro-ni.jp/90473: royal milk tea = tea and milk simmered together in a small pot, milk about half; originated at a Japanese tea room around Showa 40 (1965); no drink of that name in the UK (和製英語)",
    "Sir Thomas LIPTON press release (koubo.jp/press-release/prtimes/c79035_r102): Tea House Lipton Sanjo main store (Kyoto) is known as the first shop in Japan to serve royal milk tea, 1965 — shop's own claim → hedged 「〜とも言われます」",
    "justonecookbook.com/royal-milk-tea, sudachirecipes.com/royal-milk-tea: unlike regular milk tea, all ingredients are simmered together; similar to masala chai without spices",
    "delishkitchen.tv/articles/246, macaro-ni.jp/66911: open the leaves with a little hot water first (milk casein coats the leaves and blocks extraction), heat the milk just short of boiling"
  ],
